# {{ .Name }} ({{ .Type | title }})

Provides an API key (v2) resource. Requires API version `v3.12` or higher.

Unlike `wallix-bastion_apikey`, this v2 resource associates the key with a `profile`, which
determines the permissions the key is granted when used against the REST API.

## Example Usage

```terraform
# API key with a profile, usable from any IP
resource "wallix-bastion_apikey_v2" "ci_pipeline" {
  apikey_name = "ci_pipeline"
  profile     = "user"
  description = "Used by the CI pipeline to provision Bastion resources"
}

# API key restricted to specific source IPs
resource "wallix-bastion_apikey_v2" "monitoring" {
  apikey_name   = "monitoring"
  profile       = "auditor"
  ip_limitation = "10.10.11.22,10.10.33.44"
}
```

{{ .SchemaMarkdown | trimspace }}

## Usage Notes

### The `apikey` attribute

The Bastion API never returns the raw API key value, at creation or afterward — the `apikey`
attribute always reads back as a masked placeholder (`********`). Terraform can manage the key's
name, description, profile, and IP restriction, but retrieving the usable secret value requires
the Bastion web UI (or another out-of-band process) at creation time.

### Immutable Fields

`profile` and `ip_limitation` can only be set at creation time — the Bastion API rejects attempts
to change them via update. Changing either in Terraform forces recreation of the key.

### Profile

`profile` must reference an existing profile name (e.g. `user`, `auditor`,
`system_administrator`) and determines what the API key is authorized to do when used against
the REST API.

### IP Limitation

`ip_limitation` is a comma-separated list of IP addresses allowed to use the key. Leave it unset
(or empty) to allow use from any IP.

## Import

API key can be imported using its `apikey_name`, e.g.

```shell
terraform import wallix-bastion_apikey_v2.ci_pipeline testacc_APIKeyV2
```
